WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.571
The governing model for a secure
TypeScript service is that every client is

2
00:00:04.651 --> 00:00:07.494
untrusted and every request proposes an
operation.

3
00:00:07.574 --> 00:00:11.967
Begin by authenticating on the server and
constructing identity, tenant, role,

4
00:00:12.047 --> 00:00:14.696
and entitlement context from verified
sources.

5
00:00:14.776 --> 00:00:18.718
Validate every path, query, body, and
relevant header value at runtime.

6
00:00:18.798 --> 00:00:21.382
TypeScript types improve the
implementation,

7
00:00:21.462 --> 00:00:25.339
but their compile-time guarantees
disappear at the network boundary;

8
00:00:25.419 --> 00:00:29.231
a type assertion cannot transform
arbitrary JSON into verified data.

9
00:00:29.311 --> 00:00:33.640
Next, authorize the action and the
specific resource using trusted context and

10
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:34.948
authoritative facts.

11
00:00:35.028 --> 00:00:38.323
Scope database access by tenant or owner
where appropriate,

12
00:00:38.403 --> 00:00:42.796
and add explicit contextual policy for
permissions, delegation, sensitivity,

13
00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:43.458
and state.

14
00:00:43.538 --> 00:00:45.735
Authentication answers who is calling;

15
00:00:45.815 --> 00:00:48.399
authorization answers what that caller may
do.

16
00:00:48.479 --> 00:00:52.550
Neither replaces validation, which checks
input form, or business rules,

17
00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:56.765
which decide whether the requested state
transition is currently allowed.

18
00:00:56.845 --> 00:01:01.303
Keep prices, discounts, balances,
privilege flags, ownership, workflow state,

19
00:01:01.383 --> 00:01:04.937
audit values, and other sensitive results
under server control.

20
00:01:05.017 --> 00:01:08.118
Accept only the decisions the client is
entitled to make,

21
00:01:08.198 --> 00:01:11.171
calculate protected values from
authoritative data,

22
00:01:11.251 --> 00:01:13.964
and allowlist fields that an operation may
write.

23
00:01:14.044 --> 00:01:16.370
When correctness depends on shared state,

24
00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:20.327
join the condition and state change with
the appropriate transaction,

25
00:01:20.407 --> 00:01:25.059
conditional write, concurrency control,
uniqueness rule, or database constraint.

26
00:01:25.139 --> 00:01:29.468
An application check is useful, but it is
not atomic merely because it ran first.

27
00:01:29.548 --> 00:01:33.166
Finally, make enforcement reusable without
making it invisible.

28
00:01:33.246 --> 00:01:37.059
Use clear authentication boundaries,
schemas, services, policies,

29
00:01:37.139 --> 00:01:39.658
scoped data access, and database
guarantees.

30
00:01:39.738 --> 00:01:43.551
Test alternate routes and direct requests
that bypass the interface,

31
00:01:43.631 --> 00:01:47.960
including cross-tenant access,
over-posting, invalid transitions, replay,

32
00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:49.009
and concurrency.

33
00:01:49.089 --> 00:01:53.289
Observe meaningful denials and sensitive
actions without logging secrets.

34
00:01:53.369 --> 00:01:55.953
Usability controls can guide an honest
client;

35
00:01:56.033 --> 00:02:00.685
only server-side controls protect the
system from a custom client that ignores every

36
00:02:00.765 --> 00:02:01.928
assumption in the UI.

37
00:02:02.008 --> 00:02:05.626
If that custom client cannot make the
server violate its identity,

38
00:02:05.706 --> 00:02:08.356
authorization, business, or state
invariants,

39
00:02:08.436 --> 00:02:11.860
the architecture is enforcing security at
the right boundary.
